---
// Slot order: Claim · Evidence · Explore · Limits · Cite. Budget: ~850 words, 3 figures.
import Project from '../../../layouts/Project.astro';
import Claim from '../../../components/Claim.astro';
import Apparatus from '../../../components/Apparatus.astro';
import ExploreDoor from '../../../components/ExploreDoor.astro';
import JanghanWitnesses from '../../../components/figures/JanghanWitnesses.astro';
import JanghanLevels from '../../../components/figures/JanghanLevels.astro';
import JanghanBylines from '../../../components/figures/JanghanBylines.astro';
import j from '../../../data/janghan.json';
import { projects } from '../../../data/projects';

const p = projects.find((x) => x.slug === 'janghan')!;
const items = j.issue1.items + j.issue2.items;
const units = j.issue1.units + j.issue2.units;
const jsonld = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: 'Changhan (長恨, 1927): a reconstruction of the contents and a register of the attributions',
  description:
    'Reconstructed corpus, structural encoding and coded spatial evidence for the two 1927 issues of the Korean magazine 長恨, built from four independent witnesses that disagree about the contents.',
  creator: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Mohd Bilal' },
  temporalCoverage: '1927',
  inLanguage: 'ko',
  license: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/',
};
---
<Project title="長恨 (1927) — Contents and Attributions"
  description="A reconstruction of the contents of 長恨 (Changhan, 1927), a Korean magazine published by and largely written by kisaeng, from four independent witnesses that do not agree, with a register of its attributions."
  jsonld={jsonld}>
  <div class="wrap">

    <Claim project={p}
      meta={`${items} textual items in ${units} editorial units · 2 issues · ${j.contributors} bylines · ${j.witnesses} witnesses reconciled`} />

    <figure class="covers">
      <img src="/janghan/assets/jh01-cover.png" width="900" height="1470" loading="eager"
           alt="Cover of 長恨 issue 1, January 1927: a woman seated inside a birdcage, with the title in two circular seals above." />
      <img src="/janghan/assets/jh02-cover.png" width="411" height="606" loading="lazy"
           alt="Cover of 長恨 issue 2, February 1927: two women, one in hanbok and one in Western dress, holding hands beneath a rising sun." />
      <figcaption class="caption">
        <b><span lang="ko">창간호</span></b> · 10 January 1927, <span lang="ko">동무여 생각하라 조롱 속에 이 몸을</span>
        (“Comrades, think of me here inside the cage”), from the 1927 original.
        <b><span lang="ko">2월호</span></b> · February 1927, reproduced in the 2009 edition.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="section">
      <h2><span class="mark">Evidence</span>No two sources agree on what is in it</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          <span lang="ko">長恨</span> was published by <span lang="ko">長恨社</span> in Kyŏngsŏng in January and
          February 1927, with a kisaeng named as its publisher. Almost everything about its contents is
          contested. The published bibliography records {j.literature.issue1} and {j.literature.issue2}
          articles for the two issues; the Yonsei catalogue lists {j.yonsei.issue1} and {j.yonsei.issue2}
          entries; the 1927 table of contents lists a curated selection; a 2009 reprint numbers its own
          sequence.
        </p>
        <p>
          Those are not rival claims about the same thing. They count different units, chiefly poems printed
          inside other people's articles and prompts that carry several signed answers. This project holds the
          disagreement open instead of resolving it silently.
        </p>
      </div>
      <JanghanWitnesses />

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Counting the magazine depends entirely on what you agree to count, so the reconstruction records it
          at three levels: a <b>bibliographic entry</b> is a record in an external catalogue, a witness to the
          contents and never this project's own unit; an <b>editorial unit</b> is an item as the magazine
          presents it, one article, one column, or one prompt with its answers, and there are {units} across
          both issues; a <b>textual item</b> is a single continuous text with its own title or byline, and
          there are {items}, of which {j.nested_items} sit nested inside a parent unit. Keeping the levels
          apart is what makes the witnesses comparable at all.
        </p>
      </div>
      <JanghanLevels />

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          On that footing the attribution question becomes answerable, and the answer is the finding this
          project carries. Of the {items} texts, {j.attribution.gisaeng} are kisaeng-attributed: they appear
          under the names, pen names or self-descriptions of women from the profession the magazine was about.
          But a byline is evidence that a text was published under a name, not that a particular person wrote
          it. Only {j.certainty.confirmed} identities across the whole corpus are confirmed;
          {j.certainty.probable} are probable, {j.certainty.carried_over_unchecked} are carried over from an inherited
          baseline without independent check, and {j.certainty.unresolved} remain unresolved.
        </p>
      </div>
      <JanghanBylines />

      <Apparatus label="A complication kept in view">
        <p>
          A kisaeng is named as publisher and most contributions are kisaeng-attributed, but the editorial work
          was done in practice by Ch'oe Hak-song (<span lang="ko">최학송, 崔鶴松</span>), a male writer
          associated with proletarian literature. <span lang="ko">長恨</span> is therefore neither
          straightforwardly written by kisaeng nor simply a magazine speaking for them. This project does not
          settle that by choosing a phrase: it records what a byline evidences and how secure each
          identification is, so the question stays open to study.
        </p>
      </Apparatus>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2><span class="mark">Explore</span>The evidence itself</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The research interface is generated directly from the frozen corpus, so it cannot drift from the
          data. Every item, contributor and place opens to the evidence behind it, and each carries its corpus
          id and which of the four witnesses attests it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ExploreDoor href={p.explore!.href} label={p.explore!.label} blurb={p.explore!.blurb} />
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2><span class="mark">Limits</span>What this project does not claim</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <ul class="limits">
          <li>
            <b>No text and no page images are published.</b> The 2009 edition is in copyright
            (<span lang="ko">보고사</span>) and the 1927 scan's terms are unrecorded. What is published is
            metadata, structure, attribution and coded evidence.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Two layers of transmission.</b> All machine-readable text comes from an optical reading of the
            2009 reprint, which itself modernised spacing. Claims depending on the 1927 text's spacing,
            lineation or exact word counts are not made.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Issue 2 is a working layer.</b> No 1927 scan of it is known to survive, so page register is
            unknown throughout and the 2009 edition is its only text witness.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>The spatial question is at evidence stage, and is a separate study.</b> The reconstruction was
            built to make one answerable: how places are distributed across the authors, genres and textual
            voices of <span lang="ko">『장한』</span>. {j.place_mentions} mentions across {j.places} places have
            been coded for Issue 1 only, 33 of them unmappable, and competing explanations remain confounded.
            No answer is offered here, and nothing in this project depends on it.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="small muted">
          Full method, coding scheme, versions, datasets and licence are on the
          <a href="/projects/janghan/data/">data page</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>
</Project>
<style>
  .covers { margin: 0 0 40px; display: flex; gap: 28px; align-items: flex-start; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .covers img { display: block; height: auto; max-height: 340px; width: auto; max-width: 46%; }
  .covers figcaption { flex-basis: 100%; max-width: var(--w-measure); }
  .limits { max-width: var(--w-measure); }
  .limits li { margin-bottom: 12px; }
  @media (max-width: 640px) { .covers img { max-width: 100%; max-height: 260px; } }
</style>
